{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/periodic-field-inference.md", "content": "# Periodic-field hypothesis from heterogeneous demos

When a demo appears unrelated to another by palette, dimensions, or local geometry, first inspect whether each input nevertheless encodes an ordered sequence or lattice that is expanded into a full periodic output. For a periodic candidate, independently derive the period, symbol/color order, direction, and phase/origin from the input markers and output. Verify the complete generated grid against every demo, including row/column transitions; a visually plausible periodic fill is not evidence by itself. Heterogeneity alone is not grounds either to dismiss a shared abstract rule or to repeatedly request demonstrations without a discriminating question.

This is a diagnostic technique, not a solved task-specific mapping. In the reviewed stream, the demos included sparse color tracks and an already periodic output; the test likewise contained diagonal color tracks, but no transfer rule was rigorously established before the next demo request. Future analyses should extract and validate the proposed sequence-to-field mapping before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/periodic-field-inference.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/speculative-submission-after-demo.md", "content": "# Avoiding speculative submission after a demo

A returned demonstration pair is evidence to analyze, not permission to extrapolate from a salient resemblance. Record the exact changed and unchanged cells, then check whether the rule is uniquely supported for the current test. If an assumed stamp, anchor, orientation, or source-retention rule is not established by the pair, do not submit a guessed grid. Name at least two plausible hypotheses and request another pair only if their predictions differ in a way that can change the test answer. Analyze every newly returned pair before acting again. This is especially important after a failed submission: changing only one guessed coordinate or preserving a different portion of the candidate is not meaningful recovery unless new evidence supports it.
